WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.793
Last episode showed where your data lives.

2
00:00:01.793 --> 00:00:07.000
This one is the real walkthrough: compress it, move it, restore it on a new machine — and one gotcha that trips people up.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.157
Step one, compress the folder.

4
00:00:09.157 --> 00:00:10.739
On Linux, tar dot g z.

5
00:00:10.739 --> 00:00:12.824
On Windows, Compress-Archive.

6
00:00:12.824 --> 00:00:18.000
Either way you end up with one archive file that holds your whole setup.

7
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.590
Step two, move that archive to the new machine and extract it into place.

8
00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:28.672
Then re-enter your keys by hand — they live only in the OS credential store, not inside the folder, so the archive genuinely doesn't carry them.

9
00:00:28.672 --> 00:00:30.000
That's expected, not a bug.

10
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.158
Worth saying plainly: this is the whole app in one folder.

11
00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:41.000
There's no separate database server, no cloud account holding your history hostage — one archive of one directory is a complete, working backup.

12
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.165
The gotcha: device identity is per-machine.

13
00:00:43.165 --> 00:00:51.022
A copied install keeps the old device's mesh identity, and if both machines happen to run at once, the mesh gets confused about which one is actually which.

14
00:00:51.022 --> 00:00:55.000
The fix is simple — re-pair from the new machine, and give it its own identity.

15
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.228
That's the full deployment picture.

16
00:00:56.228 --> 00:01:01.000
To close out the advanced track, next episode pulls every privacy and security point from the whole series into one reference checklist.

